[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m int main() { int x = 0; scanf("%d", &x); driver(x); return 0; }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `driver` memcpy's `x`'s four object-representation bytes into `char raw[4]` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `print_hex` prints them as lower-case `%02x`, followed by one `'\n'`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Exit status is always 0; stderr is always empty.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Enumerated input classes (all branch points in the C)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The only conditional behavior lives inside the single `%d` conversion; the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `print_hex` loop always runs exactly `sizeof(int)` = 4 times.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Class | Example input | C behavior |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Input failure (EOF before any non-whitespace) | `""`, `"\n"`, `"   "`, `/dev/null`, closed stdin | `scanf` returns `EOF`, `x` keeps its initialiser `0` → `00000000` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Matching failure | `"abc"`, `"-"`, `"+"`, `"--5"`, `"- 5"`, `".5"`, `"\0\0 5"`, `"\xff\xfe 9"`, `"\xc2\xa0 5"` | `scanf` returns `0`, nothing stored, `x` stays `0` → `00000000` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Success, in-range | `"0"`, `"1"`, `"-1"`, `"42"`, `"+5"`, `"007"` | value stored, little-endian bytes printed |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Leading whitespace skipped across newlines | `"\n\n9"`, `" \t\n\r\n 7"`, 8 KiB of spaces then digits | whitespace consumed, then converted |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Only the first token read | `"1 2"`, `"12abc"`, `"1.5"`, `"1,234"`, `"0x10"`, `"1e9"` | conversion stops at first non-digit; remainder of stdin never read |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `int` boundaries | `2147483647`, `-2147483648` | exact |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Past `int`, inside `long` | `2147483648`, `4294967295`, `2^32`, `2^33` | `strtol` yields the full `long`; the store through `int *` truncates to the low 32 bits |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Past `long` (ERANGE) | `9223372036854775808`, `2^64`, 5000 nines | `strtol` saturates to `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` *first*, then truncates → `ffffffff` / `00000000` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Leading zeros | `"0000…005"`, 10 000 zeros then `5` | digits, not an octal prefix; do not count toward overflow |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Arguments | `driver 99 --help` | `main()` takes none; ignored |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **None.** Every enumerated input above, plus a randomized sweep of 4 000 inputs
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m drawn from the alphabet `0-9 + - . , e x _ a b c ' \t \n \r \v \f \0 \xff` and a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m boundary sweep around 0, 2^8, 2^16, 2^24, 2^31, 2^32, 2^63, 2^64, 10^19 and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 10^20 (each ±2, with and without a sign), produced byte-identical stdout,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte-identical stderr and identical exit status.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The pre-existing Rust translation already reproduced the two behaviors most
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m likely to diverge:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **`scanf` crosses newlines while skipping leading whitespace** (unlike
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `fgets`). `Input::peek`/`bump` skip all six C-locale whitespace bytes,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    including `\n`, `\v` and `\f`, before looking for a sign.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **Two-stage overflow: saturate at `long`, then truncate to `int`.** glibc's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `%d` runs `strtol`, which clamps to `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` on ERANGE; the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    assignment through `int *` then keeps only the low 32 bits. Accumulating in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `i128` and clamping to `i64` *before* the `as i32` cast is what makes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `9223372036854775808` print `ffffffff` rather than `00000000`. A naive
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `i32`-parse-and-clamp translation would differ here on many inputs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Latent divergence corrected (not observed on this target)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `driver` originally used `x.to_le_bytes()`, which hard-codes little-endian.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The C uses `memcpy(raw, &x, sizeof(x))`, i.e. the *native* object
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m representation. Changed to `x.to_ne_bytes()`. On the x86-64 test target the two
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m are identical, so this fixed no observed mismatch, but it is the faithful
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation of `memcpy` and keeps the programs in agreement on a big-endian
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m build.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Not divergent, checked explicitly
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * stdout redirected to a closed descriptor or to `/dev/full`: both exit 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (the C ignores `printf`'s return value; the Rust ignores the `io::Result`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * stdin closed (`0<&-`): both treat it as input failure → `00000000`, exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Non-UTF-8 and embedded-NUL stdin: handled byte-wise on both sides, no panic.
