[1mZero output mismatches.[22m The existing Rust already handled the two things most likely to break: [38;5;10mscanf[0m skipping newlines while hunting for the first digit, and glibc's two-stage overflow where [38;5;10mstrtol[0m clamps to [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m [3mbefore[23m the store through [38;5;10mint *[0m truncates — which is why [38;5;10m9223372036854775808[0m prints [38;5;10mffffffff[0m, not [38;5;10m00000000[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One change made to [38;5;10msrc/main.rs[0m: [38;5;10mdriver[0m used [38;5;10mx.to_le_bytes()[0m, hard-coding little-endian, where the C's [38;5;10mmemcpy[0m takes the native representation. Switched to [38;5;10mto_ne_bytes()[0m. Identical on x86-64, so it fixed no observed diff, but it is the faithful translation.[0m[0m
